# Milestone: M3
# Covers: a strand calls sleep(1ms); it is removed from the ready queue,
#         inserted into the sleep queue (sched_strand_block with
#         USTRAND_REASON_SLEEP), and resumed after the timer fires.
#
# blocked: lone-sleeper wakeup not observable via urbi_step + advance-clock
# matrix-row: temporal-sleep
#
# The `## host: advance-clock <ms>` directive (v0.11.4-cat-f) installs a
# virtual monotonic clock over vm->host_time_us, which is the prerequisite
# the original T39 note called for.  Time-literal lex + sleep() (T43) also
# shipped.  But the sleep-queue WAKEUP is still not observable from a fixture:
#
#   sched_post_dispatch (src/sched/usched_cooperative.c, "Step 3: Sleep-queue
#   wake") is the only code path that wakes a strand whose wake_us has passed,
#   and it runs ONLY inside urbi_step's dispatch loop body
#   (src/vm/ustep.c ~126).  That loop runs `while strand_runnable_count > 0`
#   and dispatches sched_pick_next().  When the sole strand is sleeping,
#   ready_head is NULL so sched_pick_next returns NULL and the loop breaks
#   BEFORE sched_post_dispatch — yet strand_runnable_count stays 1 (the parked
#   REPL loader strand), so urbi_step returns URBI_STEP_RUNNING forever and
#   never fires the clock-driven wake.  Empirically confirmed: after
#   `## host: run sleep(100ms)` then `advance-clock 200` then `step`, the
#   sleeper remains on sleep_q with wake_us=100000 < now=200000 and is never
#   resumed (ready_head stays NULL, runnable stays 1, step stays RUNNING).
#
# Activating this fixture needs a runtime change — a pre-dispatch-loop
# sleep-queue wake in urbi_step, or reconciling the parked-loader-strand
# runnable-count so a lone sleeper drives the loop — which is out of scope
# for the test-harness-only T39 work.  The equivalent sleep block/unblock
# path is unit-tested directly via the scheduler API.
